     "content": "### The Verdict\n\n**Do not accept yet, and do not walk away\u2014you should negotiate.**\n\nThis is a **strong initial offer** for a Google L6 Product Manager, but because you were passively recruited, are happy at Meta, and are a high-performing IC6, **you have maximum leverage**. Google rarely puts their best possible offer on the table in the first round.\n\n---\n\n### Compensation Breakdown & Comparison\n\nGoogle standardly uses a **front-loaded vesting schedule** (33% / 33% / 22% / 12%) for initial GSU grants.\n\n| Component | Your Current Meta Comp | Google Initial Offer (L6) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 *(Range max: $271k)* |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 *(15%)* | $47,000 *(20%)* |\n| **Target Cash** | **$276,000** | **$282,000** |\n| **Equity (4-Yr Total)** | ~$1,000,000 ($250k/yr) | $1,080,000 |\n| **Year 1 Equity Vest** | ~$200,000 *(Upcoming 12 mo)* | **$356,400** *(33% of $1.08M)* |\n| **Year 1 Total Comp** | **~$476,000** | **~$638,400** |\n| **4-Year Annual Avg TC** | **~$526,000** | **~$552,000** *(excl. refreshers)* |\n\n#### Key Financial Insights\n1. **Year 1 & 2 Upside:** Due to Google\u2019s 33/33/22/12 front-loaded schedule, your actual Year 1 and Year 2 compensation will be ~**$638k/yr**, providing an immediate ~$112k/yr boost over your Meta run rate.\n2. **The \"Year 3 Cliff\":** In Years 3 and 4, your initial grant drops to $237.6k (22%) and $129.6k (12%). Google relies on annual refreshers to fill this gap, but as the recruiter noted, refreshers depend on company performance cycles and manager calibration.\n3. **Base Salary Deficit:** Google is offering $235k base, which is **lower than your current $240k base**, despite the job range going up to $271,000.\n4. **Missing Sign-on Bonus:** Leaving unvested stock and an established track record at Meta carries transition risk. Google frequently offers a sign-on bonus to offset this.\n\n---\n\n### What to Ask For (Your Counter-Offer)\n\nSince you are not in a rush to leave, frame your counter around bridging the base gap, securing guaranteed cash upfront (sign-on), and maximizing equity:\n\n| Component | Google Initial Offer | What to Counter With | Target Settlement |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$260,000** | $245,000 \u2013 $250,000 |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | $40,000 \u2013 $60,000 |\n| **Equity (GSUs)** | $1,080,000 / 4 yrs | **$1,250,000 / 4 yrs** | $1,150,000 \u2013 $1,200,000 |\n\n---\n\n### How to Frame the Negotiation\n\nBecause you did not seek out this role, you can negotiate from a place of warmth, enthusiasm, and low pressure. \n\n#### Suggested Talking Points / Script\n\n> *\"Thank you for walking me through the details. I\u2019ve really enjoyed getting to know the team and the scope of the role, and I can see myself making a significant impact here.*\n>\n> *I want to be transparent: because I wasn\u2019t actively looking and have a great trajectory and established equity at Meta, making a move has to make clear financial sense. Looking closely at the numbers, the base salary is slightly below what I currently earn ($240k), and with no sign-on bonus, leaving my current vesting timeline and annual bonus cycle carries some risk.*\n>\n> *If we can get the base salary to **$260,000**, add a **$75,000 sign-on bonus**, and increase the equity to **$1.25M**, I would be thrilled to sign immediately.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Qualitative Factors to Weigh Before Signing\n\n* **PM Scope & Autonomy:** Meta PMs generally operate with higher autonomy and direct engineering decision-making power, but with higher performance pressure (PSC). Google PMs often navigate more cross-functional consensus and stakeholder management.\n* **WLB vs. Velocity:** Google generally offers a more sustainable work-life balance, while Meta often moves faster with quicker promotion cycles.\n* **Retirement Match:** Google offers a 50% match on 401(k) contributions up to the IRS maximum with immediate vesting, alongside access to Mega Backdoor Roth options.",
