    "message": {
     "content": "**Yes, you should accept this offer.** \n\nThis is an exceptional outcome. Securing a **$200,000 cash sign-on bonus** alongside a base increase to **$240,000** completely eliminates your transition risk and turns this into a top-of-market L6 package.\n\n---\n\n### The Updated Numbers (Google vs. Meta)\n\nGoogle's front-loaded equity vesting schedule (33% / 33% / 22% / 12%) combined with the $200k sign-on bonus makes your Year 1 compensation substantially higher than what you would make staying at Meta:\n\n| Component | Stay at Meta (Next 12 Mos) | Google Revised Offer (Year 1) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | **$240,000** |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 *(15%)* | **$48,000** *(20%)* |\n| **Year 1 Stock Vest** | ~$200,000 | **$356,400** *(33% of $1.08M)* |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | $0 | **$200,000** |\n| **Total Year 1 Comp** | **~$476,000** | **~$844,400** |\n\n* **Year 1 Net Gain:** **+$368,400** in total pre-tax earnings.\n* **4-Year Cumulative Baseline:** At Google, your baseline 4-year earnings (excluding any merit raises or stock refreshes) will be **~$2.43M** (~$608,000/yr average), compared to **~$2.10M** at Meta.\n\n---\n\n### Why You Should Stop Here and Sign\n\n1. **They fully solved your unvested equity concern:** You asked to be made whole for the $200k unvested Meta stock, and they gave it to you in **guaranteed cash**, while keeping the full $1.08M GSU grant untouched.\n2. **Top-of-Band Sign-On:** For an L6 Product Manager, sign-on bonuses typically range between $50k and $100k. Pushing Google to $200,000 means they stretched their compensation approval authority to close you.\n3. **Diminishing Returns:** The recruiter\u2019s statement that \"this is our best offer\" is credible given the size of the sign-on concession. Pushing further risks causing negotiation fatigue or having the offer pulled.\n\n---\n\n### 3 Final Things to Check Before Signing\n\n1. **Sign-On Payment & Clawback Terms:** Check whether the $200k is paid out as a single lump sum in your first 1\u20132 paychecks or split across Year 1 and Year 2. Review the clawback clause\u2014standard terms require repayment if you voluntarily resign within 12 to 24 months (ensure it is pro-rata, not a flat 100% repayment).\n2. **Start Date Timing:** If you have an impending Meta bonus payout or quarterly vest date coming up within the next few weeks, pick a start date at Google that allows you to collect it before handing in your notice.\n3. **Role & Team Scope:** Ensure the specific product area and team at Google in SF align with your long-term product interests, as the compensation package is officially as good as it gets for this level.",
